<article class="env-lemma" id="00NJ">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/00NJ">Lemma <span data-tag="00NJ">10.105.4</span>.</a> Any localization of a catenary ring is catenary. Any localization of a Noetherian universally catenary ring is universally catenary. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $A$ be a ring and let $S \subset A$ be a multiplicative subset. The description of $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(S^{-1}A)$ in Lemma <a href="/tag/00E3" data-tag="00E3">10.17.5</a> shows that if $A$ is catenary, then so is $S^{-1}A$. If $S^{-1}A \to C$ is of finite type, then $C = S^{-1}B$ for some finite type ring map $A \to B$. Hence if $A$ is Noetherian and universally catenary, then $B$ is catenary and we see that $C$ is catenary too. This proves the lemma. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>